= ​Juan Carlos I abdicates!

After quite a few years of hard-to-defend scandals, the image of the monarchy is at a low point in Spain. From pictures of the King hunting down elephants in Botswana during the worst parts of the 2008 financial crisis to diplomatic embarrassments, talks about secret deals with the Saudi monarchy, and endless gossip scandals, there's little redeeming qualities left. Now, in an attempt to clean this image, King Juan Carlos I has communicated his will to abdicate. For that, a law is set to be readied for him in the Congreso to ensure his continued legal protection and borderline immunity.

His successor to the throne: his son, now-King Felipe VI. His name does not bring any good memories to Catalans or Valencians, as he was named after Felipe V, who brutally stomped any resistance in the former Crown of Aragon in his push for centralization and the elimination of historical rights and autonomy. In any case, his reign is set to not be short of challenges, with increased republican sentiment and the very system that puts him on the throne today being put into question, who is to say what history will say about Felipe VI.

- @post_event: Of course we don't even get to vote for him...